/*
Problem: Minimum Deletions to Make String Balanced (LC 1653)
Topic: Greedy / Dynamic Programming

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

A balanced string has all 'a'
before all 'b'.

While scanning the string:

• Count how many 'b' have been seen.

When an 'a' appears after some 'b's,
there are two choices:

1. Delete this 'a'.
   Cost = previous deletions + 1

2. Delete all previous 'b's.
   Cost = number of previous 'b's

Choose the cheaper option.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

Traverse the string.

If current character is 'b':
    Increase bCount.

If current character is 'a':
    ans = min(ans + 1, bCount)

Return ans.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

"aababbab"

Process:

a -> ans = 0

a -> ans = 0

b -> bCount = 1

a -> min(1,1) = 1

b -> bCount = 2

b -> bCount = 3

a -> min(2,3) = 2

b -> bCount = 4

Answer:

2

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Whenever an 'a' appears after one or
more 'b's, the string becomes
unbalanced.

To fix it we either:

• Delete this 'a'

or

• Delete every previous 'b'

The minimum of these two choices
always gives the optimal answer.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

Single traversal of the string.

Space : O(1)

Only two variables are used.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Greedy + DP State Compression
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int minimumDeletions(string s) {

        int bCount = 0;
        int ans = 0;

        for (char ch : s) {

            // Count previous 'b'
            if (ch == 'b') {
                bCount++;
            }

            // Current 'a' after some 'b'
            else {

                // Option 1: delete this 'a'
                // Option 2: delete all previous 'b'
                ans = min(ans + 1, bCount);
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
